# --- active: the Trino programme (adopted 2026-09-13) ---
# Trino as a fifth backend and a fourth SQL dialect, from dialect/printer through
# to the ledger. T1-T5 are listed in DEPENDENCY order and must not be reordered:
# T2's audit legs execute SQL against a live engine, so T1's HTTP client and
# compose tier come first; T4 consumes T3's state-residency verdict, which decides
# which maintenance techniques are reachable at all; T5 proves T1-T4 on a real
# pipeline over real rows.
#
# Unlike the BigQuery and Databricks spines, NOTHING here needs a cloud account, a
# credential, a bill or a human provisioning gate — only Docker. So every outcome
# is fully loop-grindable with no split driver. The one discipline that carries
# over: a phase needing the live coordinator must emit <<PHASE_BLOCKED>> when
# scripts/trino-env.sh cannot reach it, NEVER skip green. An audit or conformance
# leg that skips is indistinguishable from one that passes — the same hole an
# unset DUCKDB_LIB_DIR opened.
#
# Connector choice is load-bearing, not incidental: Iceberg (REST catalog + MinIO)
# is the one profile, because the connector — not Trino — decides the write
# surface. MERGE / UPDATE / DELETE / CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE exist on Iceberg and
# do not exist on Hive, so T3 and T4 depend on that choice.
#
# Ruling of 2026-09-13, recorded in T3's and T4's decision logs: Trino's
# transaction support is taken as EQUIVALENT TO SPARK'S, and some incremental
# features are accepted as unsupported on Trino for now. Iceberg has Delta's
# atomicity shape (per-table commits, no cross-table transaction), so T3 starts
# from Spark's state-residency column — all five correctness structures a
# permanent, reasoned `no` — and T4 therefore runs FULLY DEGRADED, following
# maintenance_conformance_spark rather than the DuckDB leg. A phase that discovers
# genuine cross-table atomicity on Iceberg must escalate it, not absorb it.
